# Uppsala University

> Uppsala University (Uppsala universitet) is Sweden's oldest and one of Scandinavia's leading research universities, founded in 1477. Located in Uppsala, Sweden, with a second campus in Visby (Gotland), the university offers world-class education and research across humanities, social sciences, law, medicine, pharmacy, and science and technology. With approximately 50,000 registered students, 7,539 employees, and 16 Nobel Prize laureates among its alumni, Uppsala University consistently ranks among the global top 100. Its mission is to conduct education and research of the highest quality and relevance to make a long-term difference in society.

Uppsala University is a comprehensive public research university governed by the Swedish state. It is organised into three disciplinary domains — Humanities and Social Sciences, Medicine and Pharmacy, and Science and Technology — comprising nine faculties and approximately 60 departments. The university motto is "Gratiae veritas naturae" (Truth in the grace of nature). The Vice-Chancellor is Anders Hagfeldt. Key historic figures associated with Uppsala include Carl Linnaeus (taxonomy), Anders Celsius (temperature scale), Svante Arrhenius (Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1903), and Dag Hammarskjöld (Nobel Peace Prize 1961). Svante Pääbo (Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2022) is a notable alumnus. The university is ranked #87 in the QS World University Rankings 2027. Annual turnover is SEK 9,235 million; approximately 58% of research funding comes from external sources. The university trains more than 3,000 physicians, nurses and medical staff annually in cooperation with Uppsala University Hospital (Akademiska sjukhuset).

Uppsala University Library, founded in 1620, is Sweden's largest research library with 11 subject libraries including Carolina Rediviva. The university hosts several museums open to the public: the Museum of Evolution (over 5 million natural history specimens), Gustavianum (Uppsala University Museum, with Egyptian mummies, Viking-era artefacts, the Augsburg Art Cabinet and an anatomical theatre from 1663), the Museum of Medical History, and the Linnaeus Museum. The Linnaeus Garden and Botanical Garden are connected to the university's Linnaean heritage. The student nation system — unique student organisations with social and welfare functions — is a centuries-old Uppsala tradition.

